# gitleaks baseline — KNOWN HISTORICAL LEAKS ONLY
#
# ============================================================================
#  DELETE THIS ENTIRE FILE once the credentials below have been ROTATED and
#  the git history has been rewritten (e.g. git filter-repo). Until then,
#  every CI run would otherwise fail on secrets that are already public, so
#  the job would be permanently red and therefore ignored.
#
#  Rotating is the actual fix. This file only stops the alarm from being
#  useless in the meantime — it does NOT make the repository safe.
# ============================================================================
#
# Each entry is a gitleaks fingerprint: <commit>:<path>:<rule-id>:<line>.
# They are pinned to the specific historical commit, path, rule and line, so:
#   * these nine known findings are suppressed
#   * the SAME credential reintroduced in ANY new commit still fails CI
#   * no path, rule or time range is blanket-ignored
#
# Deliberately no secret values appear in this file — fingerprints are
# positional only. Re-committing the tokens we are trying to remove would
# defeat the point.
#
# Regenerate after a history rewrite with:
#   gitleaks git -c .gitleaks.toml --report-format json --report-path /tmp/gl.json .
#   python3 -c "import json;[print(f['Fingerprint']) for f in json.load(open('/tmp/gl.json'))]"

